= Distributional semantics — Technical =

== Distributional matrix ==
Let X be a term–context matrix.

A weighting such as positive pointwise mutual information can emphasize unexpectedly strong associations:

<pre>
PMI(w,c) = log P(w,c) / (P(w)P(c))
PPMI(w,c) = max(PMI(w,c), 0)
</pre>

Singular-value decomposition or related factorization can produce dense latent vectors.

== Predictive embeddings ==
Predictive objectives learn representations by estimating a target from context or context from a target.

The learned representation geometry is objective-dependent. Cosine proximity is useful because it suppresses vector magnitude, not because cosine is a universal semantic metric.

== Contextual language models ==
Transformers produce token representations conditioned on an input sequence.

For token i at layer ℓ:

<pre>
hᵢ^(ℓ) = TransformerLayerℓ(h₁^(ℓ-1), ..., hₙ^(ℓ-1))
</pre>

Different layers and pooling procedures encode different mixtures of lexical, syntactic, contextual, and task-relevant information.

== Evaluation ==
Evaluation includes intrinsic word similarity, analogy, probing, lexical substitution, word-sense tasks, semantic textual similarity, retrieval, and downstream prediction.

Intrinsic benchmark correlation can be informative but is not equivalent to semantic validity across domains.

== Bias and identifiability ==
Representations inherit properties of corpora and optimization objectives. Rotation-equivalent or otherwise transformed embedding spaces can preserve predictive behavior while changing individual coordinates, so dimensions should not be interpreted as semantic features without evidence.

